From 93d6d282dd30ae23f3ca41d0d8470c02a9ebfa0f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: readmet3xt-lab <238408149+readmet3xt-lab@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 01:26:46 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 80/81] A stand-in for reflections without rays
 (VICTORY_AMBIENT_SPECULAR=<share>[,<sky>], off by default)

Without the structure builder every reflection ray the screen-space march leaves misses, and CS_RayMiss
(06bb6e728c7c99bf) writes the sky's light into the rays' texture (#38965, 853x480 R11G11B10, capture
C:\dev\captures\haze2): mean RGB (6.7, 10.9, 18.9) against (0.72, 0.44, 0.50) for the lit scene in the same
units, 100-1000x the room it should show and blue (the user's "reflections show a bright colour"; eyes glowing
blue-white, blue blotches on floors). Not a cleared or stale texture, and the cube maps are the sky's.

With the switch, right after each CS_RayMiss of opaque surfaces and of the alpha layer (not water: its first
root constant is 2), a pass of ours reflects the view direction about the normal made from the depth CS_RayMiss
itself reads (table (0,1000): #46202, a 1706x960 D32 target drawn in list 167 of the frame, before the trace)
and writes share x the light stand-in's directional estimate along it (ambient.rs, from
CS_CacheScreenSpaceRadiance), mixed with <sky> of the title's colour and never brighter than the estimate;
sky pixels (depth at the far plane) keep the title's value. CS_ResolveScreenSpace then writes its own hits
over it, so screen-space reflections stay. Expected cost under 0.15 ms a frame at 1706x960 (three dispatches).
A line every 10 s says it ran.

Written by a helper; built and tested at the merge into local/drive. A side finding to check: the diffuse
stand-in's depth slot (ambient::DEPTH, (0,464)) may name the stencil plane of #6481 after the
_ReflectiveCaustics variant 34fb51b8a0312e82.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit 2db59d48134a2804a7e2edb80553dc7e61165880)
